% ============================================================================
% spectral-class-order — each stellar spectral class letter's rank in the
% hottest-to-coolest sequence, per the SAME already-cited NASA sentence
% (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `spectral-classes.adj`
% (`spectral_class_color(spectral_class, color)`, the color NASA assigns each
% of the seven main-sequence spectral classes — o → blue, b → blue_white,
% a → white, f → yellow_white, g → yellow, k → orange, m → red). That table's
% own header already quotes, verbatim, ONE continuous NASA sentence that
% lists the seven class letters IN ORDER and states the direction of that
% order — a fact the color-only schema had no room for:
%
%   "Astronomers use these characteristics to classify main sequence stars
%    into categories by color and temperature: O (blue), B (blue-white),
%    A (white), F (yellow-white), G (yellow), K (orange), and M (red), from
%    hottest and biggest to coolest and smallest."
%
% The letter SEQUENCE (O, B, A, F, G, K, M) fixes each class's rank; the
% trailing clause ("from hottest and biggest to coolest and smallest") fixes
% the DIRECTION — rank 1 is the hottest/biggest class, rank 7 the coolest/
% smallest. This is the SAME "list order → NUMBER column" move already
% established in this directory by `moon-phases.adj`'s own `moon_phase_order`
% table. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? spectral_class_order(g, $Order)   % 5  (our Sun's class, 5th hottest)
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `spectral_class_order(spectral_class, order)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query — the engine returns the rank WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on a letter that is not one of these seven
% classes (it never invents a rank). The `order` value is a NUMBER, so a
% recalled rank composes into arithmetic (how many classes separate O and M?
% 7 − 1 = 6 steps apart). Full 7/7 coverage — the single quoted sentence
% names every class's position, so no abstention is needed among the seven;
% the table abstains only on non-class atoms (`z`, `x`, a brown-dwarf label),
% the same abstention set `spectral-classes.adj` already establishes. The
% query also runs BACKWARD — bind a rank and recall its spectral class:
%
%     ? spectral_class_order($Class, 1)   % o  (rank 1 is the hottest class)
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME NASA sentence
% `spectral-classes.adj`'s own header already quotes — no new WebFetch, only
% a different schema decoding the ORDER half of the already-verified quote
% instead of the color half. `trust authoritative` — the same tier
% `spectral-classes.adj` already carries (NASA Science, a primary U.S.
% government resource).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table spectral_class_order {
    columns spectral_class, order

    row (o, 1)
    row (b, 2)
    row (a, 3)
    row (f, 4)
    row (g, 5)
    row (k, 6)
    row (m, 7)

    source "Astronomers use these characteristics to classify main sequence stars into categories by color and temperature: O (blue), B (blue-white), A (white), F (yellow-white), G (yellow), K (orange), and M (red), from hottest and biggest to coolest and smallest."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/stars/"
    trust authoritative
}
